\title{INT9: the second optimisation wave together - the merge, the gates and the guard of the published build, and where it stands against gruszka}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{benchmark}
\tag{performance}
\tag{soundness}
\tag{arena}
\tag{platform}
\tag{machine}
\tag{loader}
\tag{driver}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Integration package "INT9", 2026-11-25 (19:13 to 21:55 UTC). The second optimisation wave after the first release had seven packages, each in a private copy of the tree with gates and a guard of its own: the session tables of the machine ([rep-OPT-D](rep-OPT-D)), RLVM against gruszka unit by unit ([rep-OPT-E](rep-OPT-E), research), the loader thread ([rep-OPT-F](rep-OPT-F)), huge pages ([rep-OPT-G](rep-OPT-G)), a table of comparison results that survives sessions ([rep-OPT-H](rep-OPT-H)), the probe budget and repeated declarations ([rep-OPT-I](rep-OPT-I)), the JVM launcher on a stock OpenJDK ([rep-OPT-J](rep-OPT-J)). None had been combined with another. This report is the record of the integration, in two phases: the changes that were to go in merged into one set against the main tree, gated before the publication (sections 1 to 5); and, after the orchestrator had published the set as \commit{34e8647}, the remaining gates, the guard of [the rule for the default configuration](dec-YP0N) with gruszka in the same windows, and the benchmark rows on the published build (sections 6 to 8). What is in the default build and what is not is [the decision](dec-RHRE). [The integration of the first wave](rep-INT8) is the model for the method, and its merged build, with the level fix of [rep-INV-1](rep-INV-1) on top, is the build before this wave.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Answer}

\ol{
\li{\strong{The merged set} (sections 1 and 2): seven patches and one archive against the main tree, five changed jars. No hunk failed; two packages meet in one source file, the loader, where the repeat lookup stays in front of everything that moves the segment base and the stretches of the second pass give every record the ordinal it had. A digest of the new repeat column is the same in 28 of 28 reader configurations. The publication (\commit{34e8647}) produced exactly the jars that had been gated.}
\li{\strong{The open point of the session tables is settled} (section 3): on Init the young and old tables are not slower than the larger table sizes alone, 591.5 ms each with a control at 591.3 ms, six interleaved rounds on a quiet host; reachability and con-leche need the tables (0.890 and 0.983 of the passes with the sizes alone). The tables went in.}
\li{\strong{The gates pass, before the publication and on the published build} (sections 4 and 6), judged by official verdicts: the gate of the default configuration through the executable and through the jars - 230 of 230 published tests, 17,775 mutants with SOUNDNESS 0 and COMPLETENESS 0 (agree 15,741, declined 2,034), the hostile suite of now 1,274 rows with no wrong accept and no wrong reject (agree 1,192, tolerated 78, declined 4); the reference engine by name and the strict policy; the driver's invariance suite; the machine's own gate with the table audit on; the differential engine on the eight corpora, in which the reference engine accepts each of 1,579,800 units, the 136,567 repeats that the driver did not check among them; the arena's own procedure on its 270 tests on the shared and on a release-layout executable; and the JVM launcher on a stock OpenJDK with the same status on all 270. No wrong accept and no wrong reject was seen anywhere.}
\li{\strong{What is computed changed exactly as predicted} (section 5): with the probe budget and the repeat skip switched off, no counter of a one-worker run differs from the main tree on any corpus and launcher; the budget alone and the skip alone change what rep-OPT-I says, to the tick; together they add up (Mathlib 574,100,326 to 559,746,444 machine ticks).}
\li{\strong{One verdict changes, on hostile input, and it is a tolerated one} (section 6): the file \code{\verb~|R-e1-sib-krule-major-redex-bad~}, a row of the tolerated kind with its theorem repeated, is accepted since the repeat skip and was rejected at the repeat before. On the 1,648 hostile files with repeats the invariance suite has the six known schedule differences of three rows under a one-byte session cap, with the skip and without it, and no other.}
\li{\strong{The guard holds} (section 7). Warm pass, 8 workers, three interleaved rounds per corpus on a quiet host, against the build before the wave: the executable 6.8 \% faster in the geometric mean of the eight corpora (1.839 to 1.714 s), every corpus faster by 4.4 to 8.7 \%, Mathlib 13.14 to 12.29 s; the JVM launcher 7.7 \% faster (1.567 to 1.446 s), every corpus faster by 4.1 to 13.9 \%, Mathlib 11.11 to 10.35 s. Against the release before both waves the executable is 15 to 18 \% faster on the three largest corpora (Mathlib 14.52 to 12.29 s). No window saw a compaction stall. The level fix alone costs nothing a window resolves (Std 0.999, cslib 0.999, Mathlib 1.002).}
\li{\strong{Against gruszka}, in the same windows: the published executable needs 1.05, 1.12, 0.99, 0.21, 1.19, 1.26, 1.06 and 1.14 times gruszka's wall clock on Init, Std, cedar, reachability, con-leche, navier-stokes-euler, cslib and Mathlib (12.29 against 10.77 s); the published JVM launcher 0.97, 0.96, 0.85, 0.15, 1.02, 1.13, 0.90 and 0.96 (10.35 s): ahead of gruszka on six corpora, behind on con-leche and navier-stokes-euler. By CPU both are still behind on seven corpora (Mathlib 1.49 and 1.25 times gruszka's).}
\li{\strong{In the arena's metric} (section 6.1): Mathlib takes 1,432.5 to 1,433.2 G instructions, 2.0 \% fewer than after the first wave, and the seven large corpora 1.1 to 4.3 \% fewer. reachability takes 4.9 \% more and 17 of the 262 small tests 3.1 to 10.6 \% more, at 11 \% less CPU time in sum: the young and old tables execute more and wait less. One performance test is slower by both measures.}
\li{\strong{The benchmark rows and the optional build} (section 8): the tables and the README carry the published build - Mathlib 12.77 s as a cold process and 12.32 s per warm pass for the executable, 10.32 s per warm pass on the JVM launcher, gruszka 10.82 and 10.74 s in the same windows. The profile of the optional Oracle GraalVM build was recorded again from the published jars; that executable needs 0.91 to 0.95 times the default executable's warm pass (Mathlib 11.60 s).}
\li{\strong{Not done} (section 9): nothing on x86-64; the changes one by one on the published build; a profile of what is left of the distance to gruszka.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. What was merged, and against what}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The main tree before the wave}: [the first wave](rep-INT8) and the fix of the level store's instantiation caches ([the stale level lists](rep-INV-1), \commit{55f2db3}; \code{rlvm-level} \code{35e453f8c770}, \code{rlvm-store} \code{5328df68c01b}). A private copy of its tracked sources with a pristine twin; the twin, built whole by \src{scripts/build.sh} with \code{\verb~|--dev~}, is "the build before the wave" of this report (jars sha256 \code{300e7397de62}; executable sha256 \code{5d96873ac96f}). Its jars are byte for byte the published ones wherever the embedded build file is the same (\code{rlvm-level}, \code{rlvm-store}, \code{rlvm-export}, \code{rlvm-load}, \code{rlvm-api}, \code{rlvm-ref}, \code{rlvm-main}, \code{rlvm-admit}, \code{rlvm-nat}, \code{rlvm-cli}), so a jar is reproducible from its sources; every counter of a one-worker run is the same for this build and for the shared distribution of that hour (0 of 177 to 182 counters differ on the eight corpora).}
\li{\strong{What went in}, each as its owner's patches, none of which had been combined with another: the young and old session tables of the machine ([rep-OPT-D](rep-OPT-D); with them the entry \code{\verb~|Values.closureNew~} of the force-inline list in place of \code{\verb~|Values.closureOverNew~}); the loader thread's second pass, three patches ([rep-OPT-F](rep-OPT-F)); the default budget of an eager probe, 2^17 ticks, and the repeat skip, a declaration that repeats an accepted one word for word is admitted and not checked again, three patches ([rep-OPT-I](rep-OPT-I)); the 78 hostile exports of [rep-OPT-H](rep-OPT-H) as rows of the machine's hostile suite, without its switch, its test class and its switch sets; and the one flag that keeps the JVM launcher from starting on a stock OpenJDK ([rep-OPT-J](rep-OPT-J)), in the four files that carry it.}
\li{\strong{What did not go in}: the closed-pair table of rep-OPT-H, the huge-page mechanism of [rep-OPT-G](rep-OPT-G), a change of the session cap and the smaller alternative of rep-OPT-D (the table sizes alone; section 3), the fast and slow splits of [rep-OPT-A](rep-OPT-A).}
\li{\strong{The merged set} is seven patches, one per module in the order in which the modules are installed (\code{rlvm-export}, \code{rlvm-store}, \code{rlvm-load}, \code{rlvm-driver}, \code{rlvm-machine}, the hostile rows, the flag), and one archive with the 78 compressed exports. Applied to the main tree it gives the gated private tree file for file (2,315 files). Five jars change: \code{rlvm-export} \code{8fcc3cb37172}, \code{rlvm-store} \code{e2847c61a33c}, \code{rlvm-load} \code{6cc864470e08}, \code{rlvm-driver} \code{71da48c8c6ee}, \code{rlvm-machine} \code{1eaaf3e3655c}; of \code{rlvm-truffle} only the build file changes. The publication (\commit{34e8647}) installed the five modules from tested snapshots and got exactly these hashes.}
\li{\strong{The merged private build}: the copy with the set applied, built the same way (jars \code{70e7d9767d42}; executable sha256 \code{a70fae57de84}; inline lists with 269 forced and 81 never-inlined methods, as before). Sections 3 to 5 ran on it and on the build before the wave; sections 6 to 8 ran on the published build.}
\li{\strong{Host.} aarch64, 10 Cortex-X925 and 10 Cortex-A725, 121 GB. Every time in seconds is from a \src{bench/quiet.py} window on the 8 measurement cores, with the other cores idle unless a table says otherwise; gates and count runs are not timings and ran behind \src{bench/slot.sh}. No Oracle GraalVM was used except for the optional build of section 8.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. The merge: what overlapped, and how it was resolved}

\ul{
\li{\strong{No hunk failed.} Every patch applied to the main tree singly, and they applied one after the other: the loader patch of the repeat skip on top of the loader patch of the second pass with offsets only (12 lines in \code{\verb~|Loader.java~}) and with two lines of fuzz in the module's README, where the two edit adjacent paragraphs. \code{\verb~|LoadTarget~} and \code{\verb~|StoreTarget~} are touched by the repeat skip only. In the whole set two packages meet in one source file, \code{\verb~|Loader.java~}, and in the READMEs of \code{rlvm-load} and \code{rlvm-store}.}
\li{\strong{The one file, read.} The repeat lookup of rep-OPT-I stands in \code{\verb~|Loader.declare~} directly behind the reservation of the constant and in front of everything that writes the segment; it compares the two root ordinals with \code{segBase}, which only the end of a segment assigns. The stretch of rep-OPT-F (\code{\verb~|Loader.exprStretch~}) reads \code{segBase}, advances the record count and the segment length, and gives a record the ordinal that the record-by-record path gives it. So the key of the lookup - kind, list of universe-parameter names, hint, type ordinal, value ordinal - does not depend on whether the records came in a stretch, one by one or as tapes of the scanner threads, and metadata refuses the stretch in both versions.}
\li{\strong{The one file, measured.} A digest of the new column, every pair of a unit and the unit it repeats, written for this purpose in the manner of the store digest: identical in 28 of 28 reader configurations (one, two and three scanner threads, chunks of 256 bytes to 4 MiB, both policies) on 17,158 small files, the 263 arena tests below 100 MB, 847 prelude mutants, the eight corpora and the Cedar slice; in every loaded file the column, the walk over it and the loader's own count agree. The repeats it finds are those of rep-OPT-I: Init 5,948, Std 8,661, cedar 15,429, reachability 220, con-leche 4,570, navier-stokes-euler 13,331, cslib 30,445, Mathlib 59,900. The loader's oracle of rep-OPT-I, which finds the repeats of every test file again from the reference records, runs in the merged suite of \code{rlvm-load} over the stretches of three kinds (85 tests).}
\li{\strong{Modules of one package are their owner's candidate}: the merged \code{rlvm-export} is the gated candidate of rep-OPT-F file for file, \code{rlvm-driver} that of rep-OPT-I, the five changed sources of the machine those of rep-OPT-D and \code{\verb~|MachineEngine.java~} that of rep-OPT-I; the manifest of the hostile suite is that of rep-OPT-H (1,274 rows), and the archive holds its 78 exports byte for byte.}
\li{\strong{The flag.} \code{\verb~|-XX:+UnlockExperimentalVMOptions -XX:-UseJVMCICompiler~} in place of \code{\verb~|-XX:-UseJVMCICompiler~}: on the GraalVM JDK of the build 531 VM flags are listed under both lines and one has another final value, a randomised address; the launcher of the build before the wave ends with status 1 on Eclipse Temurin 25.0.5.1+2 before the checker starts, the merged launcher accepts Init there and on the GraalVM JDK, and the Truffle probe of the build passes.}
\li{\strong{One sentence corrected afterwards.} A comment of the driver's settings (\src{rlvm-driver/src/main/java/rlvm/driver/RunConfig.java}) said that the setting \code{repeats} cannot change a verdict. It can, on one hostile file of the tolerated kind (section 6 and [the decision](dec-RHRE)); the comment says so since this report. No code changed with it.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. The young and old tables against the table sizes alone: Init}

\p{rep-OPT-D had left one point open: in the one window that compared its candidate with the base at the candidate's table sizes, the passes of Init were 2 to 7 \% longer at the same CPU time, and a smaller alternative existed, the sizes alone. It was settled before the set was fixed, in one window of 507 s (19:22:24 to 19:30:51 UTC) on an otherwise idle host: the executable, 8 workers, six interleaved rounds with the order rotated, one process per variant and round, the first pass of a process left out. Variants: the build before the wave; the same executable with \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.sizes=14,16,16,14,13,10~} (the sizes alone, as rep-OPT-D measured them); the build before the wave with the patch of rep-OPT-D; and the second variant once more, as a control of what the window resolves.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Corpus (rounds x later passes)}\th{main tree, ms}\th{sizes alone}\th{young and old tables}\th{sizes alone, control}\th{tables / sizes: passes (range of the six rounds)}\th{tables / sizes: CPU}\th{control / sizes: passes}}
\tr{\td{Init (6 x 11)}\td{610.8}\td{591.5}\td{591.5}\td{591.3}\td{1.0000 (0.987 to 1.015)}\td{1.0008}\td{0.9997}}
\tr{\td{reachability (6 x 9)}\td{452.2}\td{461.2}\td{410.5}\td{453.2}\td{0.890 (0.827 to 0.978)}\td{0.899}\td{0.982}}
\tr{\td{con-leche (6 x 5)}\td{1382.3}\td{1379.2}\td{1356.2}\td{1381.8}\td{0.983 (0.953 to 1.016)}\td{0.981 (every round 0.974 to 0.988)}\td{1.002}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Init}: the tables and the sizes alone are the same, 591.5 ms each, with the control at 591.3; the CPU time of a pass is 4.267, 4.270 and 4.268 s. The 2 to 7 \% of rep-OPT-D are not there on a quiet host with six rounds.}
\li{\strong{reachability and con-leche need the tables}: 11 \% and 1.7 \% shorter passes than with the sizes alone, 10 \% and 2 \% less CPU; the two copies of the sizes differ by 1.8 \% and 0.2 \% (reachability's passes scatter, its CPU ratios do not: 0.864 to 0.933 in the six rounds).}
\li{Peak resident memory is the same in the three (Init 384, 384, 374 MiB; reachability 736, 736, 738; con-leche 1,359, 1,358, 1,350).}
\li{\strong{So the tables went in}, as planned, and the alternative did not. The load average that the window's wrapper logged at its start, 8.59, was the tail of this work's own builds, which had ended 50 seconds earlier; no other process was running.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. The gates of the merged private build, before the publication}

\p{All on the merged private build of section 1, through its own launchers and with no \code{\verb~|-X~} option unless the row names one. Every oracle is an official verdict. No wrong accept and no wrong reject was seen, in the build before the wave or in the merged one.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Gate}\th{Result}}
\tr{\td{The suites of the touched modules, in the private copy against its own repository}\td{\code{rlvm-level} 72 tests (1 skipped), \code{rlvm-export} 82 (1 skipped: the Mathlib differential, which has a switch of its own and was not run; the module is the candidate of rep-OPT-F, which ran it), \code{rlvm-store} 75 (1 skipped), \code{rlvm-load} 85, \code{rlvm-driver} 116 (3 skipped), \code{rlvm-machine}: the fast group 424 (3 skipped), everything not tagged slow 455 (4 skipped): 0 failures, 0 errors. The whole reactor compiles with its tests}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|SHostileSlowTest~}}\td{2 tests: 56 switch sets by 1,274 rows, 0 unexpected verdicts and 0 known findings open under every set; under \code{default} 76 tolerated accepts and 12 rejections repaired by the re-check, the counts of rep-OPT-H for its rows without its switch}}
\tr{\td{Store equality: the differential digest of the loader's tests (the unchanged tool of [rep-OPT-B](rep-OPT-B) and rep-OPT-F), merged build against the build before the wave}\td{IDENTICAL in 28 of 28 configurations: the 25 of rep-OPT-F and the strict policy on the three lists of large files; each also equals the one-thread digest of its list and the digests that rep-OPT-F had taken of the main tree before the level fix. The digest reads the unit rows, the constants, the terms, the blocks, the names and the claims and never the new column of repeats, so the one intended difference of the store is invisible to it; section 2 has the digest of that column}}
\tr{\td{The gate of the default configuration through the executable (\code{\verb~|scripts/gate.sh --defaults --corpus all --hostile --native~})}\td{PASS: 230 of 230 published tests; 17,775 mutants: SOUNDNESS 0, COMPLETENESS 0, agree 15,741, declined 2,034, no error, no run at the judge's limit; the differential self-test caught; hostile suite, 1,274 rows: agree 1,192, tolerated accepts of kind (d) 78, wrong accept 0, wrong reject 0, declined 4}}
\tr{\td{The same through the jars}\td{PASS with the same counts; no hostile row has another verdict through the jars than through the executable. Of the 1,196 rows of the main tree: agree 1,124, tolerated 68, declined 4, the counts of the release; of the 78 new rows: agree 68, tolerated 10 (the eight rows of the box under its shapes and the two eager rows that rep-OPT-H lists for the default set without its table)}}
\tr{\td{The eight corpora with 8 and with 4 workers, both launchers}\td{32 of 32 runs ACCEPT, exit status 0. Declines of the machine (attempts) 0, 2, 2, 0, 4, 32, 22 and 128, the units decided by the reference engine 0, 1, 1, 0, 2, 16, 11 and 64, no rejection of the machine; \code{\verb~|driver.repeatsAwaited~} 0 in every run; 97.3 to 99.5 \% of the repeats skipped on Init, Std, cedar, navier-stokes-euler, cslib and Mathlib, 94.1 to 97.3 \% of con-leche's and 86 to 91 \% of the 220 of reachability; the others came up while their original had no status and were checked}}
\tr{\td{The strict policy (\code{\verb~|-X policy=strict~}), 8 workers, both launchers}\td{16 of 16 runs ACCEPT, every unit accepted, 0 declined, 0 repeats skipped (Mathlib: 721,845 of 721,845 units; the loader finds the 59,900 repeats, nobody reads them)}}
\tr{\td{The table audit of rep-OPT-D (\code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.tableaudit=true~}: every reset audits the young and old tables of the session that ends), JVM launcher, 8 workers, default cap}\td{8 of 8 corpora accept, no audit failure (Mathlib: 17 s more CPU than a plain run)}}
\tr{\td{Cost only, and exactly the predicted differences: every counter of a deterministic run}\td{section 5}}
}

\p{Peak resident set of one cold process, MiB, the executable with 8 workers / the JVM launcher with 8 workers: Init 358 / 692, Std 590 / 1,363, cedar 733 / 1,598, reachability 714 / 1,442, con-leche 845 / 1,450, navier-stokes-euler 1,224 / 2,142, cslib 1,545 / 2,330, Mathlib 3,585 / 4,738 (not timings: the runs shared the host with the count runs). Under the strict policy the executable peaks at 13.0 GiB on Mathlib and 5.2 GiB on con-leche, the JVM launcher at 13.3 and 7.8 GiB.}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. The counters of a deterministic run: nothing but what the reports predict}

\p{One worker makes a run deterministic (file order, batch load). Every count that a run reports - the machine's ticks, records, run words, pairs, calls and memo hits per judgment, resets and declines, the eager and code-block counters, the reference engine's units and steps, the driver's and the loader's counts; times, bytes and the allocator left out - and the unit trace, unit by unit, in five configurations per corpus and launcher: the build before the wave; the merged build with both changes of rep-OPT-I switched off (\code{\verb~|-X repeats=check -X machine.eager-probe=4194304~}); with the probe budget alone; with the repeat skip alone; and without any option. 88 runs with the control of section 1, the largest at 4.3 GiB resident. The executable and the JVM launcher report the same counts in all 40 pairs of runs, so one table:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Corpus}\th{counters compared}\th{differ with both changes of OPT-I switched off}\th{differ with the probe budget alone}\th{differ with the repeat skip alone}\th{differ in the merged build}\th{of them statistics that are new}\th{not attributed}}
\tr{\td{Init}\td{177}\td{0}\td{1}\td{75}\td{78}\td{3}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{Std}\td{180}\td{0}\td{1}\td{80}\td{83}\td{3}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{cedar}\td{180}\td{0}\td{1}\td{82}\td{85}\td{3}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{177}\td{0}\td{1}\td{70}\td{73}\td{3}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{182}\td{0}\td{19}\td{81}\td{84}\td{3}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{182}\td{0}\td{1}\td{82}\td{85}\td{3}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{182}\td{0}\td{76}\td{83}\td{98}\td{3}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib}\td{182}\td{0}\td{76}\td{84}\td{97}\td{3}\td{0}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The session tables and the loader's second pass compute what the main tree computes.} With both changes of rep-OPT-I switched off no counter differs on any corpus, and every unit has its ticks and its outcome; the merged build only reports three statistics more (\code{\verb~|load.repeatUnits~}, \code{\verb~|driver.repeatsSkipped~}, \code{\verb~|driver.repeatsAwaited~}).}
\li{\strong{The probe budget alone} is rep-OPT-I, section 2.3, to the tick. On Init, Std, cedar, reachability and navier-stokes-euler the one counter that differs is the setting itself. con-leche: 19 counters, 4,063,232 ticks fewer, all in unit 13,027, which was cut and declined before and is cut earlier (4,196,461 to 133,229 ticks). cslib and Mathlib: 76 counters each, 7,850,591 and 7,850,590 ticks fewer, all in the four units of \code{\verb~|Batteries.Data.Char.Basic~} (units 96,222, 219,779, 219,799, 219,801 and 321,319, 362,538, 362,559, 362,561), which go from accepted by the machine to declined by it and decided by the reference engine in 15,760 steps; the machine's declines rise by 8 attempts, the reference engine's units by 4.}
\li{\strong{The repeat skip alone} is rep-OPT-I, section 3.7, to the tick: the units not checked are the repeats the loader found, their ticks are gone, and the other units pay a little of it back, because a warm session no longer holds what the check of a repeat left in it. No unit has another outcome; 70 to 84 counters differ per corpus.}
\li{\strong{Together they add up.} Every counter that differs between the main tree and the merged build differs under one of the two changes alone or is one of the three new statistics, and for every counter that is a sum the joint difference is the sum of the two single differences. One counter is not a sum: \code{\verb~|machine.eager.maxTicks~} on cslib, the largest eager run, is 3,218,191 in the main tree, 136,770 with the budget alone and 136,771 in the merged build, where the session of that unit is one tick different.}
}

