}

\subtree{
\title{6. The published build: the remaining gates}

\p{The set was published as \commit{34e8647}: the five modules installed from tested snapshots with the hashes of section 1, the shared distribution rebuilt at 20:00 UTC (jars sha256 \code{f5517aa135d2}; the executable sha256 \code{01737cb59df8}; the JVM launcher with the machine's heap line \code{\verb~|-Xmx7g -Xmn1g~}). At the publication the gate of the default configuration through the executable passed on it with the counts of section 4, and the arena definition built and ran end to end. The gates below ran on that distribution between 20:06 and 20:37 UTC, the runs of the corpora on a byte-identical copy; none had to be run twice. No wrong accept and no wrong reject.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Gate}\th{Launcher, configuration}\th{Result}}
\tr{\td{Default configuration, the executable (\code{\verb~|gate.sh --defaults --corpus all --hostile --native~}; run at the publication)}\td{\code{rlvm}, no option}\td{PASS: 230 / 230; 17,775 mutants SOUNDNESS 0, COMPLETENESS 0, agree 15,741, declined 2,034, no error; hostile 1,274 rows: agree 1,192, tolerated 78, wrong accept 0, wrong reject 0, declined 4}}
\tr{\td{Default configuration, the jars (\code{\verb~|gate.sh --defaults --corpus all --hostile~})}\td{\code{rlvm-jvm}, no option}\td{PASS: 230 / 230; 17,775 mutants SOUNDNESS 0, COMPLETENESS 0, agree 15,741, declined 2,034, no error and no run at the judge's limit; self-test of the differential path caught; hostile 1,274: agree 1,192, tolerated 78, wrong accept 0, wrong reject 0, declined 4}}
\tr{\td{The reference engine by name (\code{\verb~|gate.sh --corpus all --hostile~})}\td{\code{rlvm-jvm}, \code{\verb~|-X engine=reference~}, batch load, one worker}\td{PASS: 230 / 230; 17,775 mutants SOUNDNESS 0, COMPLETENESS 0, agree 15,741, declined 2,034, no error; self-test caught; hostile 1,274: agree 1,260, tolerated 6, wrong accept 0, wrong reject 0, declined 8 (with the 1,196 rows of before: 1,182, 6 and 8: the reference engine gives the official verdict on each of the 78 new rows)}}
\tr{\td{The strict policy (\code{\verb~|gate.sh --corpus bugs --hostile -X policy=strict~})}\td{\code{rlvm-jvm}, \code{\verb~|-X policy=strict~}, batch load, one worker}\td{PASS: 230 / 230; 2,692 mutants of the corpus \code{bugs} SOUNDNESS 0, COMPLETENESS 0, agree 2,579, declined 113, no error; hostile 1,274: agree 1,255, tolerated 0, wrong accept 0, wrong reject 0, declined 19 (before: 1,177, 0 and 19)}}
\tr{\td{The driver's invariance suite, the machine as primary engine: every file under 9 schedules (batch with 1, 2, 4 and 8 workers; a session cap of one byte with 1 and 4 workers; pipelined with a publication every 1, 7 and 64 units and 1, 4, 8 workers)}\td{the published jars}\td{PASS: 15,183 files (the 231 published tests, 12,260 mutants of the tutorial tests, 2,692 of the corpus \code{bugs}): 0 wrong, 0 differences between schedules, 0 declines that only a pipelined run has; 478 mutants declined alike in every schedule (413 and 65), the counts of the release}}
\tr{\td{The invariance suite on the 1,648 hostile files with repeats of rep-OPT-I (41 written for the repeat skip, 1,193 rows of the suite with their last declaration repeated, 414 with it repeated under another kind), with the skip and with \code{\verb~|repeats=check~}}\td{the published jars}\td{0 wrong, 0 declined, 6 schedule differences in each of the two runs, the same 6 and the ones rep-OPT-I has: the three rows of kind T that attack warm sessions (\code{\verb~|R-s3-wc-warm-pair-reused-bad~}, \code{\verb~|R-s3-wc-warm-term-reused-bad~}, \code{\verb~|R-s3-wc-warm-term-reused-reset-bad~}) are rejected at their original unit under the one-byte session cap, with 1 and with 4 workers, and accepted in the seven other schedules. No other file differs between its schedules. \code{\verb~|R-e1-sib-krule-major-redex-bad~} has one verdict in all 9: accept with the skip, reject at the repeat (unit 13) with \code{\verb~|repeats=check~}}}
\tr{\td{The machine's M8 gate of the default set, all parts, the table audit of rep-OPT-D on in every lane (\code{\verb~|report.sh gate --switches default --session-mib 1 -Drlvm.machine.tableaudit=true~})}\td{the main tree of \code{rlvm-machine}, in process}\td{PASS WITH DECLINES, as before the wave. Differential against the reference engine per declaration: the 230 tests (15,845 engine checks), Init (57,938), Std (100,096), reachability (4,224), the Cedar slice (8,064): SOUNDNESS 0, COMPLETENESS 0, one unit of Std declined by the rule of finding E1-F6. Tier equivalence with the generic tier: 0 different outcomes. Convention equivalence (host depth 0 and unbounded against 128): 0 different declarations. The audit lanes \code{\verb~|poison,scopeaudit,maskaudit~} and \code{\verb~|memocheck,shortcheck~}: the same outcomes, no declaration ended by an audit. The pipeline: 230 of 230 verdicts right, MACHINE-INCOMPLETE 0, WARM-ANOMALY 0, no disagreement. No table audit failed in 729 s of the gate}}
\tr{\td{The differential engine through the launcher (\code{\verb~|-X engine=diff~}: the reference engine beside the machine on every unit, the repeats that were not checked included; a difference declines the run), 8 workers, the eight corpora}\td{\code{rlvm} and \code{rlvm-jvm}}\td{16 of 16 runs ACCEPT. In each launcher the reference engine checked 1,579,800 units - every unit the workers were given: Init 57,938, Std 100,096, cedar 144,098, reachability 4,224, con-leche 36,274, navier-stokes-euler 124,850, cslib 397,608, Mathlib 714,712 - and accepted every one; disagreements 0, MACHINE-INCOMPLETE 0, WARM-ANOMALY 0. Of them 136,567 (executable) and 136,502 (JVM launcher) were repeats that the driver had admitted without a check; the machine declined 0, 2, 2, 0, 4, 32, 22 and 128 attempts}}
\tr{\td{The eight corpora with 2 and with 3 scanner threads (\code{\verb~|-X scan-threads=N~}: the reader's stretches arrive at the loader as tapes), 8 workers}\td{\code{rlvm}}\td{16 of 16 runs ACCEPT, the machine's declines as with one scanner thread (0, 2, 2, 0, 4, 32, 22, 128)}}
\tr{\td{The arena's own procedure, 270 tests}\td{\code{rlvm}, the run line of the arena definition; the shared executable and a release-layout executable of the published jars}\td{no wrong accept, no wrong reject, no error, no timeout on both executables: good 148 / 148 accepted, bad 92 / 92 rejected, either 11 accepted / 17 rejected / 2 declined (the two on which the official kernel does not terminate); status and exit code of all 270 tests are the same for the two executables and the same as for the build of the first wave; Mathlib 1,432.5 to 1,433.2 G instructions (section 6.1)}}
\tr{\td{The JVM launcher on a stock OpenJDK: Eclipse Temurin 25.0.5.1+2 through \code{\verb~|RLVM_JAVA~}, the launcher's flag line as shipped}\td{\code{rlvm-jvm}}\td{Init, Std and cslib ACCEPT with 8 workers. The arena's 270 tests with the arena's run line in an arena-sized container, once on the GraalVM JDK of the build and once on Temurin: good 148 of 148 accepted, bad 92 of 92 rejected, either 11 accepted, 17 rejected, 2 declined on both; 270 of 270 tests with the same status and the same exit code on the two JDKs, no error, no timeout (not timed: 5 performance cores each)}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The counts are those of the release wherever the wave did not mean to change them}: the published tests, the four mutant corpora, the invariance suite, the M8 gate. The hostile suite has 78 rows more, with 10 more tolerated accepts in the default configuration and none in the two configurations of the reference engine.}
\li{\strong{The one verdict the wave changes} is outside the suite: the hostile file \code{\verb~|R-e1-sib-krule-major-redex-bad~} of rep-OPT-I, a row of the tolerated kind with its theorem repeated, is accepted by the default configuration since the repeat skip and was rejected at the repeat before, which is the official verdict. The statement of a repeat is the statement of its original, so nothing underivable is accepted; the strict policy rejects the file, and \code{\verb~|-X repeats=check~} gives the old verdict. [The decision](dec-RHRE) takes it under the practical policy.}
\li{\strong{Which engine decides which unit} changed in two ways, and the differential engine is the gate for both: the units that the driver admits on the word of their original, 5 to 12 \% of a corpus, are each accepted by the reference engine when it is asked; and the four units of cslib and of Mathlib that the smaller probe budget hands to the reference engine are accepted by it, as the machine accepted them before.}
}

\subtree{
\title{6.1 The arena's own procedure on the published build}

\p{As in [rep-AR2](rep-AR2) and rep-INT8: the run line of the arena definition (\src{scripts/arena/rlvm.yaml}), verbatim and with no option, over the arena's complete test set (148 good tests with the eight corpora among them, 92 bad, 30 either) in one arena-shaped container (8 performance cores, 16 GB, no network), the tests one after the other with the arena's time limits, instructions counted around every run; each suite inside one quiet-core window (21:25:48 to 21:28:32 UTC; 80 s per suite). Twice: on the shared executable of the publication (development layout; sha256 \code{01737cb59df8}) and on a release-layout executable built for this purpose from the published jars by \code{\verb~|scripts/build.sh --published~}, with the third-party jars checked against \src{scripts/arena/deps.sha256} (one file of the same size, sha256 \code{332a43afd9e9}). Then Mathlib alone, twice per executable, each run in a fresh container.}

