\tr{\td{Init}\td{68.3}\td{68.2}\td{70.4}\td{-3.1 \%}\td{4.1}\td{0.60}\td{367}}
\tr{\td{Std}\td{118.2}\td{118.3}\td{121.5}\td{-2.7 \%}\td{7.5}\td{1.05}\td{533}}
\tr{\td{cedar}\td{167.3}\td{167.0}\td{172.4}\td{-2.9 \%}\td{10.5}\td{1.48}\td{748}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{15.8}\td{15.8}\td{15.1}\td{+4.9 \%}\td{1.1}\td{0.45}\td{729}}
\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{148.6}\td{148.7}\td{155.3}\td{-4.3 \%}\td{8.4}\td{1.45}\td{812}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{415.8}\td{415.9}\td{420.2}\td{-1.1 \%}\td{25.6}\td{3.47}\td{1,380}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{479.9}\td{479.8}\td{499.2}\td{-3.9 \%}\td{33.7}\td{4.56}\td{1,549}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib}\td{1,432.9}\td{1,433.2}\td{1,462.3}\td{-2.0 \%}\td{96.1}\td{12.77}\td{3,577}}
\tr{\td{the 262 other tests together}\td{621.1}\td{621.8}\td{595.3}\td{+4.3 \%}\td{}\td{}\td{}}
\tr{\td{\strong{all 270 tests}}\td{\strong{3,467.8}}\td{3,468.9}\td{3,511.7}\td{\strong{-1.2 \%}}\td{235}\td{}\td{}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Verdicts}: the table of section 6; no test used a quarter of its time limit, none was above 4 GiB resident.}
\li{\strong{Mathlib alone}, the arena's ranking input: 1,432.83 and 1,432.46 G instructions on the published executable (CPU 96.2 and 96.0 s, wall clock 12.76 and 12.71 s, 3.5 GiB resident) and 1,432.58 and 1,433.22 G on the release-layout executable (12.73 and 12.77 s, 3.6 and 3.7 GiB), where the build of the first wave had 1,462.3 G: 2.0 \% fewer.}
\li{\strong{By instructions the wave is not a gain everywhere.} Seven corpora need 1.1 to 4.3 \% fewer instructions. reachability needs 4.9 \% more, and of the 262 other tests 17 need more than 2 \% more (3.1 to 10.6 \%; sixteen of them performance tests of one large unit) and none 2 \% fewer; the 239 tests below 0.5 G are unchanged in sum (3.06 G). On the same 17 tests the CPU time goes down, 45.4 to 40.4 s in sum, and reachability goes from 1.29 to 1.11 s: in a large session the young and old tables execute more instructions, a second probe for a key made of old things, and wait less for memory. One test is slower by both measures, \code{\verb~|perf/magma-list-deep-n36~}: 68.1 to 74.6 G instructions and 6.5 to 7.2 s of CPU. rep-OPT-D had not taken the instruction count; the maintainer's metric is the wall clock, the arena's is this one.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. The guard of the published build, and gruszka in the same windows}

\p{The rule of [the decision on the default set](dec-YP0N): on the eight corpora the geometric mean of the warm passes goes down and no corpus is more than 3 \% slower, over at least three interleaved rounds, on the executable and on the JVM launcher. Measured between 20:37 and 21:16 UTC on a host on which nothing else ran: one quiet-core window per corpus on the 8 measurement cores; in every window the executable and the JVM launcher of the build before the wave (section 1: the first wave and the level fix) and of the published build, on the three largest corpora also the executable of the release before both waves; the variants interleaved round by round (in order, reversed, in order), three rounds, 8 workers, a run without options but the worker count and the passes; and gruszka with its arena run line and 8 workers in the arena container on the same cores, two runs before every round and two after the last. A cell is the mean over the rounds of the median later pass of one process (the first pass of a process is left out); for a pass shorter than 0.5 s, reachability, the mean of the later passes. CPU is the process CPU of a later pass; peak RSS the largest of the rounds. All 105 processes accepted in every pass.}

\subtree{
\title{7.1 The executable}

