\ul{
\li{\strong{The executable against gruszka in these windows}: 1.04, 1.08, 0.99, 0.20, 1.24, 1.27, 1.08 and 1.15 times gruszka's time for a warm pass (the tables after the first wave: 1.15, 1.21, 1.06, 0.24, 1.28, 1.31, 1.17, 1.23), and 1.07 to 1.35 times for one cold process on seven corpora. The guard windows of section 7, where gruszka ran eight times per corpus beside three rounds of the executable, give 1.05, 1.12, 0.99, 0.21, 1.19, 1.26, 1.06 and 1.14; a pass of about a second moves by up to 5 \% between windows, con-leche here.}
\li{\strong{The JVM launcher} is at 0.95, 0.95, 0.86, 0.13, 0.97, 1.10, 0.90 and 0.96 of gruszka's second run (after the first wave: 1.04, 1.03, 0.89, 0.18, 1.06, 1.14, 0.98, 1.02): ahead on seven corpora in these windows, on six in the guard windows, behind on navier-stokes-euler in both.}
\li{\strong{Against nano-lean} the executable needs 0.60 to 0.89 of the time of a warm pass and 0.60 to 0.95 of a cold process.}
\li{\strong{A cold process against a warm pass}: 2 to 15 \% longer (Mathlib 12.77 against 12.32 s).}
\li{\strong{The reference engine by name} is what it was: the wave changes its loader's inner loops and nothing of its engine. Cold processes within 2 \% of the rows after the first wave on seven corpora (Mathlib 75.01 s, there 75.98) and 5.5 \% longer on con-leche (32.23 against 30.54 s, one process each); instructions within 0.1 \% on all eight (Mathlib 7,808.6 G, there 7,804.6).}
\li{\strong{Instructions of the default configuration}, one cold process each in the arena-shaped container: 68.2, 118.2, 167.1, 15.8, 148.7, 416.1, 479.9 and 1,433.2 G, the counts of the arena procedure of section 6.1. Against gruszka's counts with its arena run line, as recorded on this host: 3.0 to 3.5 times on seven corpora, 1.20 times on reachability (after the first wave 3.1 to 3.6 and 1.14).}
}

\subtree{
\title{8.1 The optional build with Oracle GraalVM}

\p{Oracle GraalVM is used by this explicitly optional build only; nothing was downloaded (the toolchain directory had it). The profile was recorded again with \code{\verb~|scripts/dev.sh profiles --published~} at 20:10 UTC: an instrumented image of the published jars (97 s), the Truffle probe, and Init once in the default configuration (1.9 s instrumented). The record of the profiles in the repository names the jars of the published build (\code{rlvm-export} \code{8fcc3cb37172}, \code{rlvm-store} \code{e2847c61a33c}, \code{rlvm-load} \code{6cc864470e08}, \code{rlvm-driver} \code{71da48c8c6ee}, \code{rlvm-machine} \code{1eaaf3e3655c}, \code{rlvm-level} \code{35e453f8c770}; profile id \code{169e607bc762}, 2.6 MB compressed). The optional executable was then built once from them (73 s; 46.4 MB, sha256 \code{591eb855f426}); its build record says "profile state: fresh". The whole command took 176 s. The same command without \code{\verb~|--published~} does not build: it compiles every module from the tree against the Truffle version of the Oracle GraalVM, and the sources of \code{rlvm-truffle} in the tree, which are newer than its published jar (5 classes differ, one is new), do not compile against that version. It failed in its Maven step and changed nothing.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Its row} (the table above): Mathlib 12.03 s cold and 11.60 s for a warm pass, Init 0.57 and 0.53 s, cslib 4.28 and 4.07 s. Against gruszka's second run its warm pass is at 0.95, 1.01, 0.91, 0.19, 1.18, 1.17, 1.00 and 1.08.}
\li{\strong{Its lead over the default executable is what it was after the first wave}: 0.91 to 0.95 times the default executable's warm pass on the eight corpora (there 0.92 to 0.98), and 0.89 to 0.95 times its cold wall clock on seven corpora with reachability at 1.07 (0.49 against 0.46 s, one process each of under half a second). The sentence of the root README was changed to these figures.}
\li{\strong{Not gated.} The optional executable accepted the eight corpora in its 16 benchmark processes; no gate and no arena procedure was run on it.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{9. What was not measured}

\ul{
\li{\strong{x86-64.} Every figure is from the aarch64 development host. The arena's runner is x86-64 with 16 GB; nothing of this wave ran there.}
\li{\strong{The changes one by one on the published build.} The guard of section 7 is the sum of the wave. What each change gives alone is in its own report, measured against a base of its own, most of them on a busy host and before the level fix; the counters of section 5 attribute what is computed, not the time.}
\li{\strong{Two builds of an image are not the same code} ([the insight](ins-TWRM)). The executable before the wave and the published one are two builds, as they have to be; the window of section 3 compared a setting inside one executable where it could (the table sizes) and two private builds where it could not. No executable was built twice to see what a second build of the same jars moves.}
\li{\strong{The optional executable} (Oracle GraalVM, profile-guided) was not gated and did not go through the arena's procedure: it accepted the eight corpora in the processes of its benchmark rows.}
\li{\strong{The JVM launcher on a stock OpenJDK} was run for verdicts only in this report: Init, Std and cslib and the 270 arena tests. Its warm pass, its counters and its build on that JDK are in rep-OPT-J, measured with the refused flag left out; with the flag pair they were not measured again. One stock build, one patch level.}
\li{\strong{The Mathlib differential test of the reader} has a switch of its own and was not run on the merged tree; the merged \code{rlvm-export} is file for file the candidate on which rep-OPT-F ran it.}
\li{\strong{The mutant corpora under the invariance suite} other than the tutorial and bugs mutants (the metadata and prelude mutants) were not run; the gates of section 6 run all four corpora through the launchers.}
\li{\strong{gruszka and nano-lean} are the builds of the baseline tables, not rebuilt; gruszka ran in every guard window, nano-lean only in the windows of the benchmark rows.}
\li{\strong{The profile of the published build.} Where the remaining distance to gruszka goes after this wave was not profiled; [the profile of the release](rep-P11) and [the unit-by-unit comparison](rep-OPT-E) are of builds before it.}
\li{\strong{Why the instruction count goes up on single large units} (section 6.1) was not profiled: that the young and old tables execute more and wait less is read from the counts and the CPU times of the arena procedure, not from a profile, and the one test that is slower by both measures was not looked at.}
\li{\strong{The peak memory of the JVM launcher on Init} is 34 MiB higher after the wave in the guard window (811 to 845 MiB) and was not explained.}
}
}

